            "text": "Builds a new array cell by cell: where the condition is true it takes the value from 'if true', elsewhere from 'if false'.\n\nThink of it as a stencil or a masked blend rather than a lookup. Feed it a comparison as the condition and you have \"use this where the reading is good and that where it is not\" across a whole array in one step - no loop, no branching. Replace bad readings with a fallback, paste one image into another through a mask, zero everything below a threshold.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.where\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ncondition:\nThe mask. Usually the result of a comparison node (>, <, ==) on an array, but any array works: a non-zero value counts as true. This is the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nif true / if false:\nThe values to choose from. Each can be an array the same shape as the condition, or a single number used everywhere - 'if false' 0 blanks every cell the condition rejects. These inlets are cold: they are stored, and used when the next condition arrives. Send them FIRST.\n\nThe node sends NOTHING until both 'if true' and 'if false' have received something. A np.where with one branch unwired stays silent and gives no sign why. If the shapes cannot be matched up (a 10 x 10 condition with a 6 x 6 'if true'), nothing is sent either; with verbose on the console names the shapes.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nselected:\nAn array the shape of the condition, each cell from one source or the other.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nnp.grid makes a 10 x 10 square of points from -1 to 1. Two pictures come out of it: 'if true' is the x of each point (dark on the left, bright on the right), 'if false' is the y (dark at the top, bright at the bottom). np.linalg.norm gives each point's distance from the centre, and < 0.75 turns that into the condition: a white disc, true inside, black outside. The result shows the left-to-right ramp inside the disc and the top-to- bottom ramp around it. Drag the number on the < node and the disc grows or shrinks.\n\nThe 't array array' fires right to left, so both pictures reach np.where before the condition does - the order matters, because only the condition makes it send.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.argwhere answers a different question about the same mask: not what values to use, but WHERE the true cells are. The comparison nodes (>, <, ...) make the condition. np.clip limits values without a mask. In PyTorch, t.masked_select (on the t.index_select page) takes out just the values where a mask is true. np.[] selects by position."
        },
